    "summary": {
      "es": "This command generates an encrypted backup of the HSM key storage table.\n\n> **Check:** Enabled in PCI HSM mode\n\n**Notes**\n\n- A complete backup sequence usually requires sending a series of BACK commands to the HSM. The number of messages required to back up a specific HSM depends on the amount of data stored.\n- To begin the backup process, send this command with a **CI** token followed immediately by a delimiter (**;**), followed by token **BJ** if desired. If you have data to back up, the HSM responds by encrypting the data under the backup key and returning the encrypted value in the response under the **CI** and **CJ** tokens. You should store the data returned in the **CI** and **CJ** tokens to be used later in the **REST: Restore Key Table** command as needed.\n- Send the **CI** token and data in the next request to the HSM. The HSM uses this data to determine where to start looking for more data to back up. If there is more data to back up, the HSM returns it in the **CI** and **CJ** tokens, encrypted under the backup key. If there is no more data to back up, the HSM responds with token **BB** followed by the literal `OK` response to indicate the backup sequence has been completed.\n- To restore the backed up information to another HSM using the **REST: Restore Key Table** command, the recipient HSM must contain the same backup key as the original HSM. If it does not, the recipient HSM responds with an error message.",
      "en": "This command generates an encrypted backup of the HSM key storage table.\n\n> **Check:** Enabled in PCI HSM mode\n\n**Notes**\n\n- A complete backup sequence usually requires sending a series of BACK commands to the HSM. The number of messages required to back up a specific HSM depends on the amount of data stored.\n- To begin the backup process, send this command with a **CI** token followed immediately by a delimiter (**;**), followed by token **BJ** if desired. If you have data to back up, the HSM responds by encrypting the data under the backup key and returning the encrypted value in the response under the **CI** and **CJ** tokens. You should store the data returned in the **CI** and **CJ** tokens to be used later in the **REST: Restore Key Table** command as needed.\n- Send the **CI** token and data in the next request to the HSM. The HSM uses this data to determine where to start looking for more data to back up. If there is more data to back up, the HSM returns it in the **CI** and **CJ** tokens, encrypted under the backup key. If there is no more data to back up, the HSM responds with token **BB** followed by the literal `OK` response to indicate the backup sequence has been completed.\n- To restore the backed up information to another HSM using the **REST: Restore Key Table** command, the recipient HSM must contain the same backup key as the original HSM. If it does not, the recipient HSM responds with an error message."
    },
