      "en": "# DDTE: Delete Diebold Table Entry\n\nFuente: [DDTE: Delete Diebold Table Entry](https://docs.futurex.com/HSM_Commands/7.6.1.x/Excrypt_commands/keys/key_tables/ddte-delete-diebold-table-entry)  \nVersión del catálogo: **7.6.1.x**  \nCapturado: 2026-10-01T08:01:19.645Z\n\n---\n\nThis command enables ongoing maintenance of the Diebold Table Storage Area, so you can remove tables which you no longer use. Removing these tables not only prevents their illicit use but also makes backup and restore functions more efficient.\n\n> **Check:** Enabled in PCI HSM mode\n\n> **Check:** Enabled in FIPS mode\n\n**Notes**\n\n- Because the Diebold Table Storage Area and the DES Key Storage Table compete for space in Excrypt device memory, deleting a Diebold table can potentially create space for 32 more DES keys. However, this is only true if you delete the Diebold table having the highest location number. For example, assume you have stored 50 Diebold tables in locations zero through 49. If you delete the table in location 25, you don’t gain any extra space for DES keys because the location you cleared falls within the memory allocated for Diebold tables only. If you delete the table in location 49, that space could then be reallocated as Key Storage Table locations.\n- Excrypt numbers Diebold table positions starting at 0 and ending at 124. If your system stores both Diebold tables and DES keys, the board doesn’t have enough memory to hold a full 125 Diebold tables. The number of available locations for more Diebold tables, expressed in decimal, is returned in the Excrypt response message, preceded by a **CK** token.\n- There is no other format version of this function.\n\n<a id=\"required-permissions\"></a>\n\n## Required permissions\n\n| Permissions | Description | Port | Min Logins |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Keys | View key and table information | Mgmt | 0 |\n\n<a id=\"token-summary\"></a>\n\n## Token summary\n\nAt-a-glance request and response tokens for this command. For accepted values, conditional rules, and usage notes, see the detailed token sections below.\n\nRequest tokens\n\n| Token | Parameter | Type | Length | Required |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `AO` | Command ID | Alphabetic | 4 | Yes |\n| `BR` | Diebold table index number to delete | Numeric | 1-3 | Yes |\n\nResponse tokens\n\n| Token | Parameter | Type | Length |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `AO` | Command Response ID | Alphabetic | 4 |\n| `CK` | Diebold table locations remaining | Numeric | 1-3 |\n\n<a id=\"request-tokens\"></a>\n\n## Request tokens\n\n<a id=\"ao-command-id\"></a>\n\n### AO - Command ID\n\n| Length | Data Type | Accepted Values |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 4 | Alphabetic | `DDTE` |\n\n<a id=\"br-diebold-table-index-number-to-delete\"></a>\n\n### BR - Diebold table index number to delete\n\n| Length | Data Type | Accepted Values |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 1-3 | Numeric | 0 - 124 |\n\n<a id=\"response-tokens\"></a>\n\n## Response tokens\n\n<a id=\"ao-command-response-id\"></a>\n\n### AO - Command Response ID\n\n| Length | Data Type | Returned Values |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 4 | Alphabetic | `DDTE` |\n\n<a id=\"ck-diebold-table-locations-remaining\"></a>\n\n### CK - Diebold table locations remaining\n\n| Length | Data Type | Returned Values |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 1-3 | Numeric | 0 - 124 |\n\n<a id=\"command-example\"></a>\n\n## Command example\n\n```\nRequest: [AODDTE;BR3;]\n\nResponse: [AODDTE;CK122;]\n```\n"
