    "id": "futurex-excs-req",
    "code": "EXCS",
    "direction": "request",
    "pairedCode": "EXCS",
    "name": {
      "es": "EXCS: Configure Excrypt command specific settings",
      "en": "EXCS: Configure Excrypt command specific settings"
    },
    "summary": {
      "es": "EXCS reads and sets the HSM-wide settings that change how other Excrypt commands behave: MAC verification strictness, PIN block translation rules, decimalization table handling, weak key loading, payment token TPK requirements, GBIC key restrictions, and several TR-31 policies. Each setting has its own token, and the value decides the operation rather than a separate mode token: `-2` resets the setting to its default, `-1` returns the current value, and `0` or `1` set it. The settings are global to the HSM, not per session, so a change persists until it is changed again and it affects every port and every connected caller. The response shape follows the operation: a `-1` query returns only that setting alongside `TY`, while a set, a reset, or a request with no setting token at all returns the full settings dump. Because a change here silently alters how unrelated commands validate their input, read the current value before setting anything.\n\n**Notes**\n\n- Sending EXCS with only the AO token returns all current setting values without modifying anything.\n- Most setting tokens follow a consistent pattern: `-2` resets to default, `-1` queries current value, `0` and `1` set specific states.\n- SCEK from slot will always be used in secure mode, regardless of the SC setting.\n- A `-1` query returns only the queried setting and `TY`. Any other operation returns the full dump, so the dump is not a confirmation that a specific value took effect. Follow a set with a `-1` query on the same token to confirm it.\n- The full dump omits `VL`, `GT`, `AP`, and `TE`. Those settings exist and are readable, but only through a `-1` query on the token itself.\n- An unrecognized setting token is ignored rather than rejected: the HSM returns the full dump with `TYY`. A mistyped token therefore looks like success, which is another reason to confirm with a `-1` query.",
      "en": "EXCS reads and sets the HSM-wide settings that change how other Excrypt commands behave: MAC verification strictness, PIN block translation rules, decimalization table handling, weak key loading, payment token TPK requirements, GBIC key restrictions, and several TR-31 policies. Each setting has its own token, and the value decides the operation rather than a separate mode token: `-2` resets the setting to its default, `-1` returns the current value, and `0` or `1` set it. The settings are global to the HSM, not per session, so a change persists until it is changed again and it affects every port and every connected caller. The response shape follows the operation: a `-1` query returns only that setting alongside `TY`, while a set, a reset, or a request with no setting token at all returns the full settings dump. Because a change here silently alters how unrelated commands validate their input, read the current value before setting anything.\n\n**Notes**\n\n- Sending EXCS with only the AO token returns all current setting values without modifying anything.\n- Most setting tokens follow a consistent pattern: `-2` resets to default, `-1` queries current value, `0` and `1` set specific states.\n- SCEK from slot will always be used in secure mode, regardless of the SC setting.\n- A `-1` query returns only the queried setting and `TY`. Any other operation returns the full dump, so the dump is not a confirmation that a specific value took effect. Follow a set with a `-1` query on the same token to confirm it.\n- The full dump omits `VL`, `GT`, `AP`, and `TE`. Those settings exist and are readable, but only through a `-1` query on the token itself.\n- An unrecognized setting token is ignored rather than rejected: the HSM returns the full dump with `TYY`. A mistyped token therefore looks like success, which is another reason to confirm with a `-1` query."
    },
    "description": {
      "es": "EXCS reads and sets the HSM-wide settings that change how other Excrypt commands behave: MAC verification strictness, PIN block translation rules, decimalization table handling, weak key loading, payment token TPK requirements, GBIC key restrictions, and several TR-31 policies. Each setting has its own token, and the value decides the operation rather than a separate mode token: `-2` resets the setting to its default, `-1` returns the current value, and `0` or `1` set it. The settings are global to the HSM, not per session, so a change persists until it is changed again and it affects every port and every connected caller. The response shape follows the operation: a `-1` query returns only that setting alongside `TY`, while a set, a reset, or a request with no setting token at all returns the full settings dump. Because a change here silently alters how unrelated commands validate their input, read the current value before setting anything.\n\n**Notes**\n\n- Sending EXCS with only the AO token returns all current setting values without modifying anything.\n- Most setting tokens follow a consistent pattern: `-2` resets to default, `-1` queries current value, `0` and `1` set specific states.\n- SCEK from slot will always be used in secure mode, regardless of the SC setting.\n- A `-1` query returns only the queried setting and `TY`. Any other operation returns the full dump, so the dump is not a confirmation that a specific value took effect. Follow a set with a `-1` query on the same token to confirm it.\n- The full dump omits `VL`, `GT`, `AP`, and `TE`. Those settings exist and are readable, but only through a `-1` query on the token itself.\n- An unrecognized setting token is ignored rather than rejected: the HSM returns the full dump with `TYY`. A mistyped token therefore looks like success, which is another reason to confirm with a `-1` query.",
      "en": "EXCS reads and sets the HSM-wide settings that change how other Excrypt commands behave: MAC verification strictness, PIN block translation rules, decimalization table handling, weak key loading, payment token TPK requirements, GBIC key restrictions, and several TR-31 policies. Each setting has its own token, and the value decides the operation rather than a separate mode token: `-2` resets the setting to its default, `-1` returns the current value, and `0` or `1` set it. The settings are global to the HSM, not per session, so a change persists until it is changed again and it affects every port and every connected caller. The response shape follows the operation: a `-1` query returns only that setting alongside `TY`, while a set, a reset, or a request with no setting token at all returns the full settings dump. Because a change here silently alters how unrelated commands validate their input, read the current value before setting anything.\n\n**Notes**\n\n- Sending EXCS with only the AO token returns all current setting values without modifying anything.\n- Most setting tokens follow a consistent pattern: `-2` resets to default, `-1` queries current value, `0` and `1` set specific states.\n- SCEK from slot will always be used in secure mode, regardless of the SC setting.\n- A `-1` query returns only the queried setting and `TY`. Any other operation returns the full dump, so the dump is not a confirmation that a specific value took effect. Follow a set with a `-1` query on the same token to confirm it.\n- The full dump omits `VL`, `GT`, `AP`, and `TE`. Those settings exist and are readable, but only through a `-1` query on the token itself.\n- An unrecognized setting token is ignored rather than rejected: the HSM returns the full dump with `TYY`. A mistyped token therefore looks like success, which is another reason to confirm with a `-1` query."
    },
