    "summary": {
      "es": "This command converts a key under a major key to under a Key Encryption Key (KEK). This command is identical to **GPKW**, except that it allows a key to be encrypted with a weaker KEK under some conditions. The KEK must be have at least 112 bits of security to be able to wrap a stronger key. See the following **Key Strengths**table for bits of security carried by each supported key type.",
      "en": "This command converts a key under a major key to under a Key Encryption Key (KEK). This command is identical to **GPKW**, except that it allows a key to be encrypted with a weaker KEK under some conditions. The KEK must be have at least 112 bits of security to be able to wrap a stronger key. See the following **Key Strengths**table for bits of security carried by each supported key type."
    },
    "description": {
      "es": "This command converts a key under a major key to under a Key Encryption Key (KEK). This command is identical to **GPKW**, except that it allows a key to be encrypted with a weaker KEK under some conditions. The KEK must be have at least 112 bits of security to be able to wrap a stronger key. See the following **Key Strengths**table for bits of security carried by each supported key type.",
      "en": "This command converts a key under a major key to under a Key Encryption Key (KEK). This command is identical to **GPKW**, except that it allows a key to be encrypted with a weaker KEK under some conditions. The KEK must be have at least 112 bits of security to be able to wrap a stronger key. See the following **Key Strengths**table for bits of security carried by each supported key type."
    },
