      "en": "HMKE re-wraps an HMAC key so it can be handed to a party that holds a Key Encryption Key. Supply the source key in `HM` as a TR-31 key block (Usage `M7`, Algorithm `H`) currently protected under the major key selected by `FS`, and supply the KEK in `AP` as either a raw cryptogram under modifier 0 of the same major key or a TR-31 KEK key block. The response `HM` is a new TR-31 block carrying the same key material under the KEK, re-encrypted and re-MAC’d, so the payload always differs from the input even when every option is defaulted. The command changes packaging only; the cleartext key never changes, which is why `AE` is identical for the input and the output.\n\nTwo optional tokens rewrite the outgoing header rather than the key: `CZ` sets the mode of use and `TE` sets the exportability. `BO` attaches TR-31 optional blocks to the exported block. In firmware 7.6.1.37 and later HMKE also exports a source key whose exportability is already `N`, and the exported block keeps `N` unless `TE` overrides it; earlier firmware silently downgraded the outgoing header to `E`. The response also reports what the HSM found inside the source block: `RG` is the bound hash algorithm and `KL` is the cleartext key length in bytes. Generate an HMAC key with [HMKG](hmkg-generate-random-hmac-key.md), bring one back in with [HMKI](hmki-import-hmac-key.md), and use [HMAC](../generate/hmac-generate-mac-hash.md) or [HMCV](../verify/hmcv-verify-hmac.md) to exercise the key itself.\n\n> **Check:** Enabled in PCI HSM mode\n\n> **Check:** Enabled in FIPS mode\n\n**Notes**\n\n- `AP` and `HM` are both required. `AP` is validated before `HM`, so a request missing both returns `AM04 ANAP TOKEN MISSING`.\n- `FS` defaults to PMK (`6`) when omitted. `FS` is a single character: a two-character value such as `FS10` returns `AM13 ANFS INVALID MESSAGE LENGTH`.\n- `AP` and `HM` must both be protected under the same major key. Selecting a major key that does not protect them fails on `AP` first: `AM48 ANAP INVALID KEYBLOCK` for a TR-31 KEK block, `AM47 ANAP INVALID MAC` for a cryptogram.\n- In firmware 7.6.1.37 and later a source key with `N` exportability exports successfully and the outgoing header keeps `N`.\n- `TE` overrides the outgoing exportability. Sending `TEE` against a source key whose exportability is `N` produces an exportable output block, so `TE` is the way to end a non-exportable chain deliberately.\n- `CZ` overrides the outgoing mode of use (header byte 8) without touching the key material. `AE`, `KL`, and `RG` are unchanged by `CZ`.\n- `BO` is one or more TR-31 optional blocks of the form `ID(2) + LEN(2 hex) + DATA`, where `LEN` is the block’s total length including the four-character header. The HSM inserts the blocks, increments the optional-block count, and re-pads `PB` so the overall block length stays aligned. A wrong `LEN` returns `AM73 ANBO HEADER MISMATCH`.\n- `AE` is deterministic for a given cleartext key. Compare translations by `AE`, not by the raw `HM` payload.\n- The response `AG` token is a per-session request counter assigned by the HSM. Treat it as protocol framing."
