    "id": "futurex-rsae-req",
    "code": "RSAE",
    "direction": "request",
    "pairedCode": "RSAE",
    "name": {
      "es": "RSAE: Encrypt a Symmetric Key Using a Trusted RSA Public Key",
      "en": "RSAE: Encrypt a Symmetric Key Using a Trusted RSA Public Key"
    },
    "summary": {
      "es": "This command encrypts a symmetric key using a trusted RSA public key.\n\n> **Check:** Enabled in PCI HSM mode\n\n**Notes**\n\n- The **Key slot offset** differs between the API and Excrypt. While the API recognizes PKI storage to be slots 0-99, the Excrypt Manager loads keys into slots 1-100. Therefore, for tokens that support PKI storage table indexing, the key slot in which the key is loaded using Excrypt Manager is off by one (for example, a key loaded into slot **1** on the Excrypt Manager is referenced by the API as slot **0**).",
      "en": "This command encrypts a symmetric key using a trusted RSA public key.\n\n> **Check:** Enabled in PCI HSM mode\n\n**Notes**\n\n- The **Key slot offset** differs between the API and Excrypt. While the API recognizes PKI storage to be slots 0-99, the Excrypt Manager loads keys into slots 1-100. Therefore, for tokens that support PKI storage table indexing, the key slot in which the key is loaded using Excrypt Manager is off by one (for example, a key loaded into slot **1** on the Excrypt Manager is referenced by the API as slot **0**)."
    },
    "description": {
      "es": "This command encrypts a symmetric key using a trusted RSA public key.\n\n> **Check:** Enabled in PCI HSM mode\n\n**Notes**\n\n- The **Key slot offset** differs between the API and Excrypt. While the API recognizes PKI storage to be slots 0-99, the Excrypt Manager loads keys into slots 1-100. Therefore, for tokens that support PKI storage table indexing, the key slot in which the key is loaded using Excrypt Manager is off by one (for example, a key loaded into slot **1** on the Excrypt Manager is referenced by the API as slot **0**).",
      "en": "This command encrypts a symmetric key using a trusted RSA public key.\n\n> **Check:** Enabled in PCI HSM mode\n\n**Notes**\n\n- The **Key slot offset** differs between the API and Excrypt. While the API recognizes PKI storage to be slots 0-99, the Excrypt Manager loads keys into slots 1-100. Therefore, for tokens that support PKI storage table indexing, the key slot in which the key is loaded using Excrypt Manager is off by one (for example, a key loaded into slot **1** on the Excrypt Manager is referenced by the API as slot **0**)."
    },
