    "id": "futurex-stcl-req",
    "code": "STCL",
    "direction": "request",
    "pairedCode": "STCL",
    "name": {
      "es": "STCL: Clear Certificates or Keys for a TLS Processing Pair",
      "en": "STCL: Clear Certificates or Keys for a TLS Processing Pair"
    },
    "summary": {
      "es": "Use the STCL command to clear certificates, private keys, or CRLs from a TLS (SSL2TCP) processing pair. You can target a specific processing pair by number (using TP) and selectively clear the CA certificate, host certificate, private key, or CRL, or you can clear items by their direct certificate, key, or CRL index when TP is not sent. The DK token controls whether the underlying private key or certificate is also erased from the system when using the processing pair option.\n\n> **Note:** - The DK token is used to indicate whether to delete the underlying private key or certificate when using the processing pair option.\n> - Internal secure pairs cannot be cleared while in FIPS mode.",
      "en": "Use the STCL command to clear certificates, private keys, or CRLs from a TLS (SSL2TCP) processing pair. You can target a specific processing pair by number (using TP) and selectively clear the CA certificate, host certificate, private key, or CRL, or you can clear items by their direct certificate, key, or CRL index when TP is not sent. The DK token controls whether the underlying private key or certificate is also erased from the system when using the processing pair option.\n\n> **Note:** - The DK token is used to indicate whether to delete the underlying private key or certificate when using the processing pair option.\n> - Internal secure pairs cannot be cleared while in FIPS mode."
    },
    "description": {
      "es": "Use the STCL command to clear certificates, private keys, or CRLs from a TLS (SSL2TCP) processing pair. You can target a specific processing pair by number (using TP) and selectively clear the CA certificate, host certificate, private key, or CRL, or you can clear items by their direct certificate, key, or CRL index when TP is not sent. The DK token controls whether the underlying private key or certificate is also erased from the system when using the processing pair option.\n\n> **Note:** - The DK token is used to indicate whether to delete the underlying private key or certificate when using the processing pair option.\n> - Internal secure pairs cannot be cleared while in FIPS mode.",
      "en": "Use the STCL command to clear certificates, private keys, or CRLs from a TLS (SSL2TCP) processing pair. You can target a specific processing pair by number (using TP) and selectively clear the CA certificate, host certificate, private key, or CRL, or you can clear items by their direct certificate, key, or CRL index when TP is not sent. The DK token controls whether the underlying private key or certificate is also erased from the system when using the processing pair option.\n\n> **Note:** - The DK token is used to indicate whether to delete the underlying private key or certificate when using the processing pair option.\n> - Internal secure pairs cannot be cleared while in FIPS mode."
    },
