    "id": "futurex-strq-req",
    "code": "STRQ",
    "direction": "request",
    "pairedCode": "STRQ",
    "name": {
      "es": "STRQ: Generate a Signing Request from a Pair",
      "en": "STRQ: Generate a Signing Request from a Pair"
    },
    "summary": {
      "es": "STRQ generates a certificate signing request (CSR) from an existing TLS processing pair. The CSR can be output in DER, PEM, or modulus/exponent format depending on the value of the RN token. You can include X.509 subject fields such as country, organization, and common name in the request. Use the RG token with a value of `-1` to retrieve a list of available hashing algorithms before generating the CSR. Use companion command STSC to load the signed certificate back onto the pair.",
      "en": "STRQ generates a certificate signing request (CSR) from an existing TLS processing pair. The CSR can be output in DER, PEM, or modulus/exponent format depending on the value of the RN token. You can include X.509 subject fields such as country, organization, and common name in the request. Use the RG token with a value of `-1` to retrieve a list of available hashing algorithms before generating the CSR. Use companion command STSC to load the signed certificate back onto the pair."
    },
    "description": {
      "es": "STRQ generates a certificate signing request (CSR) from an existing TLS processing pair. The CSR can be output in DER, PEM, or modulus/exponent format depending on the value of the RN token. You can include X.509 subject fields such as country, organization, and common name in the request. Use the RG token with a value of `-1` to retrieve a list of available hashing algorithms before generating the CSR. Use companion command STSC to load the signed certificate back onto the pair.",
      "en": "STRQ generates a certificate signing request (CSR) from an existing TLS processing pair. The CSR can be output in DER, PEM, or modulus/exponent format depending on the value of the RN token. You can include X.509 subject fields such as country, organization, and common name in the request. Use the RG token with a value of `-1` to retrieve a list of available hashing algorithms before generating the CSR. Use companion command STSC to load the signed certificate back onto the pair."
    },
