      "en": "TKBL unwraps a symmetric TR-31 or AKB key block (`KB`) or an RSA private or public block (`RC` or `RD`) into a raw modifier cryptogram (`BG`). Symmetric inputs require the output modifier in `AS`; RSA inputs preserve their modifier binding when `AS` is omitted. Token `AP` selects a caller-supplied KEK instead of the MFK, while `SH0` loosens header validation under dual control. Use GKBL for the reverse conversion or TKBH to change a key block header without unwrapping the key.\n\n> **Check:** Enabled in PCI HSM mode\n\n**Notes**\n\n- The option to use AKB must be enabled on the HSM.\n- `AS` is required for symmetric `KB` inputs. RSA inputs in `RC` or `RD` do not require `AS`.\n- `SH0` enables less restrictive header validation under dual control.\n- The AKB/ANSI Key Block of the KEK is optional. If none is given, the working key is under the MFK.\n- If a KEK is provided: \n\n  - The output is under the KEK modifier AS.\n  - Token **KB** should be under the KEK mod 0.\n- **KB** is always under mod 0 (MFK or KEK)."
