    "name": {
      "es": "TKTW: firmware version 7.6.1.27 and later",
      "en": "TKTW: firmware version 7.6.1.27 and later"
    },
    "summary": {
      "es": "TKTW is the point-to-point encryption pipeline command: it takes card-holder data encrypted under an incoming DUKPT data key, decrypts it, detects the data format, and returns it re-encrypted under an outgoing DUKPT data key, optionally translating an accompanying PIN block from DUKPT to a PIN Encryption Key and returning a tokenized and an HMAC form of the PAN in the same round trip. The incoming key can be supplied as a Base Derivation Key and Key Serial Number pair in `GL` and `GM`, or derived through ECDHE in firmware 7.6.1.27 and later. The response `FR` token reports the format the HSM recognized in the recovered clear data: track 1, track 2, both tracks, PAN-only manual entry, PAN plus CVV manual entry, or EMV TLV. `BS` reports the parsing and PIN-translation outcome, so a request can succeed at the protocol level and still report a data problem there. In firmware 7.6.1.37 and later, filler after a complete EMV TLV chain is skipped rather than rejected, and `PC` returns PAN+CVV manual-entry data in the Worldpay format, as three individually encrypted fields for systems that need the components separated.\n\n> **Note:** This is a newer reference for the TKTW command that adds parameters for ECDHE key derivation, available in firmware version 7.6.1.27 and later.\n\n> **Warning:** Disabled in PCI HSM Mode Pending PCI Approval\n\nThis command performs the following functions\n\n- Translates card holder data from under a DUKPT key to under another DUKPT key\n- Translates a PIN block from under a DUKPT key to under a PEK\n- Attempts to determine the format of a PAN\n- Returns an HMAC+PBKDF2 of the PAN\n- Returns a token of the PAN using FPE3.1\n- Support for ECDHE key derivation for incoming cardholder decryption is available in FW 7.6.1.27 and later.\n- Returns the same card-holder data re-encrypted in the Worldpay format, as three separator-delimited fields for manual-keyed entry, in firmware 7.6.1.37 and later. Send `PC1` to request it.\n\n**Notes**\n\n- The recovered clear card-holder data may carry trailing padding. In firmware 7.6.1.37 and later the HSM tolerates padding after the parsed EMV TLV chain, so a padded EMV sample and an unpadded one both return `BSY` from the same request structure. FW-19375 records padded samples being rejected with `BSN` or `BSC` before this change.\n- `AT` and `AU` describe the block padding applied to the encrypted data in `AK`, not the trailing filler inside the recovered clear data. Trailing filler after a complete EMV TLV chain is skipped regardless of the `AT` value, and the bytes are carried through to the outgoing ciphertext unchanged.\n- `AK` must be a multiple of the cipher block size (8 bytes for TDES, 16 bytes for AES). A length that is not a multiple returns `AM03 ANAK VALUE OUT OF RANGE`, and so does data the HSM cannot resolve to a supported format; some unresolvable data returns a `BS` parsing code instead.\n- `PC1` is honored only when the recovered data parses as PAN+CVV manual entry (`FR5`). For any other `FR` value the token is ignored and no `PC` is returned.\n- `PC1` requires an output padding mode. With `AU` absent or `AU0` the request returns `AM51 ANGH INVALID CONFIGURATION`, because each of the three fields is shorter than one cipher block and needs padding to encrypt independently.",
      "en": "TKTW is the point-to-point encryption pipeline command: it takes card-holder data encrypted under an incoming DUKPT data key, decrypts it, detects the data format, and returns it re-encrypted under an outgoing DUKPT data key, optionally translating an accompanying PIN block from DUKPT to a PIN Encryption Key and returning a tokenized and an HMAC form of the PAN in the same round trip. The incoming key can be supplied as a Base Derivation Key and Key Serial Number pair in `GL` and `GM`, or derived through ECDHE in firmware 7.6.1.27 and later. The response `FR` token reports the format the HSM recognized in the recovered clear data: track 1, track 2, both tracks, PAN-only manual entry, PAN plus CVV manual entry, or EMV TLV. `BS` reports the parsing and PIN-translation outcome, so a request can succeed at the protocol level and still report a data problem there. In firmware 7.6.1.37 and later, filler after a complete EMV TLV chain is skipped rather than rejected, and `PC` returns PAN+CVV manual-entry data in the Worldpay format, as three individually encrypted fields for systems that need the components separated.\n\n> **Note:** This is a newer reference for the TKTW command that adds parameters for ECDHE key derivation, available in firmware version 7.6.1.27 and later.\n\n> **Warning:** Disabled in PCI HSM Mode Pending PCI Approval\n\nThis command performs the following functions\n\n- Translates card holder data from under a DUKPT key to under another DUKPT key\n- Translates a PIN block from under a DUKPT key to under a PEK\n- Attempts to determine the format of a PAN\n- Returns an HMAC+PBKDF2 of the PAN\n- Returns a token of the PAN using FPE3.1\n- Support for ECDHE key derivation for incoming cardholder decryption is available in FW 7.6.1.27 and later.\n- Returns the same card-holder data re-encrypted in the Worldpay format, as three separator-delimited fields for manual-keyed entry, in firmware 7.6.1.37 and later. Send `PC1` to request it.\n\n**Notes**\n\n- The recovered clear card-holder data may carry trailing padding. In firmware 7.6.1.37 and later the HSM tolerates padding after the parsed EMV TLV chain, so a padded EMV sample and an unpadded one both return `BSY` from the same request structure. FW-19375 records padded samples being rejected with `BSN` or `BSC` before this change.\n- `AT` and `AU` describe the block padding applied to the encrypted data in `AK`, not the trailing filler inside the recovered clear data. Trailing filler after a complete EMV TLV chain is skipped regardless of the `AT` value, and the bytes are carried through to the outgoing ciphertext unchanged.\n- `AK` must be a multiple of the cipher block size (8 bytes for TDES, 16 bytes for AES). A length that is not a multiple returns `AM03 ANAK VALUE OUT OF RANGE`, and so does data the HSM cannot resolve to a supported format; some unresolvable data returns a `BS` parsing code instead.\n- `PC1` is honored only when the recovered data parses as PAN+CVV manual entry (`FR5`). For any other `FR` value the token is ignored and no `PC` is returned.\n- `PC1` requires an output padding mode. With `AU` absent or `AU0` the request returns `AM51 ANGH INVALID CONFIGURATION`, because each of the three fields is shorter than one cipher block and needs padding to encrypt independently."
    },
