      "es": "TSAK unwraps a working-key cryptogram that arrived under a restricted Key Encryption Key and returns the working key in an AKB key block encrypted under a modifier of the MFK. Supply the raw cryptogram in `BG` and the KEK in `AP` as an AKB key block whose header must carry byte 4 of `N` or `E` and whose special-restriction byte 6 is either `I` (KEK is flagged to import working keys only) or `K` (standard KEK usage that TSAK downgrades to `I` on the output). In firmware 7.6.1.37 and later the KEK in `AP` may be an AES AKB key block as well as a 3DES AKB key block. `AS` names the MFK modifier under which the output AKB is wrapped. The returned AKB header in `BH` always has special-restriction byte 6 set to `I` regardless of the incoming `AP` byte 6, and `BH` is always a 3DES AKB key block even when the KEK was an AES AKB. `AE` returns the check digits of the cleartext working key and is deterministic for identical `BG`, `AP`, and `AS`. Validation order: the HSM checks `AS` first (a request with no `AS` returns `AM04 ANAS TOKEN MISSING` before `BG` or `AP` are examined), then `BG`, then `AP`. TSAK is narrower than the generic translate commands: it requires AKB key blocks (TR-31 is not accepted in `AP`) and produces AKB output only. For general KEK to MFK translation with TR-31 support, see [TVWK](tvwk-translate-visa-working-key-for-storage.md); for MFK-wrapped working keys translated back out to a KEK-wrapped form for distribution, see the TWK family ([TWKD](twkd-translate-working-key-for-distribution.md), [TWKN](twkn-translate-working-key-for-network-transfer.md)).\n\n> **Check:** Enabled in PCI HSM mode\n\n**Notes**\n\n- `AP` must be an AKB key block. TR-31 key blocks are not accepted in this token and return `AM13 ANAP INVALID MESSAGE LENGTH`.\n- A 3DES AKB key block (version `1`) is 74 characters. In firmware 7.6.1.37 and later, `AP` also accepts an AES AKB key block (version `3`): 74 characters for AES-128 and 90 characters for AES-192. AES-256 AKB key blocks are not accepted.\n- The AKB header in `AP` must have byte 4 (counting from zero) set to `N` or `E` and byte 6 set to `I` or `K`. Byte 6 of `K` in the input produces byte 6 of `I` in the output AKB.\n- `BG` is the raw KEK-encrypted working-key cryptogram: 32 hex characters for a double-length key or 48 for a triple-length key. A 16-character `BG` returns `AM23 ANBG INVALID KEY SIZE`.\n- All three of `AS`, `BG`, and `AP` are required. The HSM validates `AS` first, so a request missing both `AS` and another token returns `AM04 ANAS TOKEN MISSING`.\n- `AE` is deterministic for identical inputs; the AKB ciphertext body and MAC in `BH` are re-randomized on every call.\n- TSAK does not produce a cryptogram output. `BH` is always an AKB key block.",
      "en": "TSAK unwraps a working-key cryptogram that arrived under a restricted Key Encryption Key and returns the working key in an AKB key block encrypted under a modifier of the MFK. Supply the raw cryptogram in `BG` and the KEK in `AP` as an AKB key block whose header must carry byte 4 of `N` or `E` and whose special-restriction byte 6 is either `I` (KEK is flagged to import working keys only) or `K` (standard KEK usage that TSAK downgrades to `I` on the output). In firmware 7.6.1.37 and later the KEK in `AP` may be an AES AKB key block as well as a 3DES AKB key block. `AS` names the MFK modifier under which the output AKB is wrapped. The returned AKB header in `BH` always has special-restriction byte 6 set to `I` regardless of the incoming `AP` byte 6, and `BH` is always a 3DES AKB key block even when the KEK was an AES AKB. `AE` returns the check digits of the cleartext working key and is deterministic for identical `BG`, `AP`, and `AS`. Validation order: the HSM checks `AS` first (a request with no `AS` returns `AM04 ANAS TOKEN MISSING` before `BG` or `AP` are examined), then `BG`, then `AP`. TSAK is narrower than the generic translate commands: it requires AKB key blocks (TR-31 is not accepted in `AP`) and produces AKB output only. For general KEK to MFK translation with TR-31 support, see [TVWK](tvwk-translate-visa-working-key-for-storage.md); for MFK-wrapped working keys translated back out to a KEK-wrapped form for distribution, see the TWK family ([TWKD](twkd-translate-working-key-for-distribution.md), [TWKN](twkn-translate-working-key-for-network-transfer.md)).\n\n> **Check:** Enabled in PCI HSM mode\n\n**Notes**\n\n- `AP` must be an AKB key block. TR-31 key blocks are not accepted in this token and return `AM13 ANAP INVALID MESSAGE LENGTH`.\n- A 3DES AKB key block (version `1`) is 74 characters. In firmware 7.6.1.37 and later, `AP` also accepts an AES AKB key block (version `3`): 74 characters for AES-128 and 90 characters for AES-192. AES-256 AKB key blocks are not accepted.\n- The AKB header in `AP` must have byte 4 (counting from zero) set to `N` or `E` and byte 6 set to `I` or `K`. Byte 6 of `K` in the input produces byte 6 of `I` in the output AKB.\n- `BG` is the raw KEK-encrypted working-key cryptogram: 32 hex characters for a double-length key or 48 for a triple-length key. A 16-character `BG` returns `AM23 ANBG INVALID KEY SIZE`.\n- All three of `AS`, `BG`, and `AP` are required. The HSM validates `AS` first, so a request missing both `AS` and another token returns `AM04 ANAS TOKEN MISSING`.\n- `AE` is deterministic for identical inputs; the AKB ciphertext body and MAC in `BH` are re-randomized on every call.\n- TSAK does not produce a cryptogram output. `BH` is always an AKB key block."
