    "summary": {
      "es": "This command translates a working key encrypted under the MFK to be encrypted under a key encryption key (KEK).\n\n> **Check:** Enabled in PCI HSM mode\n\n> **Danger:** Disabled in FIPS mode\n\n**Notes**\n\n- Instead of passing the KEK as a cryptogram or key storage table slot, this command causes the security board to look for the KEK in its KEK register, a unique register that holds only one key. It uses this register KEK value to create the encrypted working key represented in the response.\n- Although you can place tokens in most Excrypt API commands in any order, token **AS** must precede token **BG** to enable Excrypt to know what modifier to use before translating the key.\n- We provide this command for historical compatibility with other security devices. Installations using more than one KEK likely prefer using the command **TWKD**, which provides more flexibility.",
      "en": "This command translates a working key encrypted under the MFK to be encrypted under a key encryption key (KEK).\n\n> **Check:** Enabled in PCI HSM mode\n\n> **Danger:** Disabled in FIPS mode\n\n**Notes**\n\n- Instead of passing the KEK as a cryptogram or key storage table slot, this command causes the security board to look for the KEK in its KEK register, a unique register that holds only one key. It uses this register KEK value to create the encrypted working key represented in the response.\n- Although you can place tokens in most Excrypt API commands in any order, token **AS** must precede token **BG** to enable Excrypt to know what modifier to use before translating the key.\n- We provide this command for historical compatibility with other security devices. Installations using more than one KEK likely prefer using the command **TWKD**, which provides more flexibility."
    },
